open scoped TensorProduct

/-!
# Faithfully flat descent of projectivity

Let `R → S` be a faithfully flat ring map and let `M` be an `R`-module whose base change
`S ⊗[R] M` is a projective `S`-module. Then `M` is projective.

The argument is the source's. Kaplansky's theorem (Theorem 058Y) writes the projective
`S`-module `S ⊗[R] M` as an internal direct sum `⨁_{i ∈ I} Q i` of countably generated
`S`-submodules. Call an `R`-submodule `N ⊆ M` *good* if its base change `N.baseChange S` is the
subsum `⨆_{i ∈ J} Q i` of the family over some set `J ⊆ I` of indices. Goodness is preserved by
arbitrary sums of submodules, and Lemma 05A8 captures any element of `M` inside a good countably
generated submodule; so a maximal independent family of countably generated projective submodules
with good sum, produced by Zorn's lemma, must sum to `M`: otherwise an element outside the sum is
captured, the enlarged sum stays good, and its quotient by the old one is countably generated with
projective base change — a direct summand of `S ⊗[R] M` — hence projective by the countably
generated case of the theorem (Lemma 05A6), so that the quotient splits off and enlarges the
family. A module which is the internal direct sum of projective submodules is projective.

## Main results

* `Stacks.Projective.FaithfullyFlatDescent.solution`: the proof of
  `Module.Projective.of_projective_tensorProduct`.

## Implementation notes

Where the source runs a transfinite recursion along a well-ordering of `M` and appeals to the
dévissage decomposition theorem (Lemma 058V), the proof below runs Zorn's lemma on the set of
independent families of countably generated projective submodules with good supremum. The two are
the same argument: the transfinite filtration of the source is the family of partial sums of such
a family, and its successive quotients are its members. Zorn's form avoids the ordinal
bookkeeping and the choice of a well-ordering, and produces the direct sum decomposition
`M = ⨁ N` directly, in the internal form `sSupIndep` + `sSup = ⊤` that Theorem 058Y and
Lemma 058W also use.

The source's route applies Lemma 05A8 to `M/M_α` and to the induced decomposition of
`(M/M_α) ⊗_R S`. The proof below applies it to `M` itself and to the given decomposition, with
`N = R·x` the submodule generated by the element to be captured, and takes the sum with the
already-constructed submodule afterwards; this uses only that goodness is closed under sums, and
avoids transporting a direct sum decomposition across the flatness isomorphism
`(M/M_α) ⊗_R S = (M ⊗_R S)/(M_α ⊗_R S)`.

That flatness isomorphism is still needed once, in the form
`nonempty_baseChange_quotient_equiv`: the base change of `↥q ⧸ p.submoduleOf q` is
`↥(q.baseChange S) ⧸ (p.baseChange S).submoduleOf (q.baseChange S)`. It is assembled from
Mathlib's `TensorProduct.AlgebraTensorModule.tensorQuotientEquiv` (right exactness) and
`Submodule.toBaseChange.toLinearEquiv` (flatness), together with `range_baseChange`, which says
that the range of a base change is the base change of the range.

Lemma 05A8 enters as `exists_countablyGenerated_baseChange_eq_iSup_of_internal_directSum`, and
the countable generation it produces is the set-level `Submodule.IsCountablyGenerated`; the
module-level `Module.CountablyGenerated` that Lemma 05A6 consumes is reached through
`countablyGenerated_quotient_of_sup_span`, which reads a countable spanning set of `q` modulo `p`
off one of `q`.

## References

* [StacksProject 05A9](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/05A9)
-/


namespace Stacks.Projective.FaithfullyFlatDescent

open scoped TensorProduct

universe u v w

section Transport

variable {R : Type*} [Semiring R] {A : Type*} [AddCommMonoid A] [Module R A]
  {B : Type*} [AddCommMonoid B] [Module R B]

/-- Countable generation is invariant under linear equivalences. -/
private lemma countablyGenerated_of_linearEquiv (e : A ≃ₗ[R] B)
    (h : Module.CountablyGenerated R A) : Module.CountablyGenerated R B := by
  obtain ⟨s, hs, hspan⟩ := h
  refine ⟨e '' s, hs.image _, ?_⟩
  have h2 := congrArg (Submodule.map (e : A →ₗ[R] B)) hspan
  rwa [Submodule.map_span, Submodule.map_top, LinearEquiv.range] at h2

/-- Projectivity is invariant under linear equivalences. -/
private lemma projective_of_linearEquiv {R : Type*} [Ring R] {A B : Type*} [AddCommGroup A]
    [Module R A] [AddCommGroup B] [Module R B] (e : A ≃ₗ[R] B) [Module.Projective R A] :
    Module.Projective R B :=
  Module.Projective.of_split (R := R) (P := B) (M := A) e.symm.toLinearMap e.toLinearMap
    (LinearMap.ext fun _ ↦ by simp)

/-- An `iSup` over a set, read as an `iSup` over its coercion to a type. -/
private lemma iSup_coe_set {α : Type*} [CompleteLattice α] {ι : Type*} (f : ι → α) (K : Set ι) :
    (⨆ i : ↥K, f i) = ⨆ i ∈ K, f i := by
  rw [iSup_subtype]

/-- A set of submodules which is independent and sums to `⊤` is an internal direct sum. -/
private lemma isInternal_of_sSupIndep {R : Type*} [Ring R] {M : Type*} [AddCommGroup M]
    [Module R M] {𝒩 : Set (Submodule R M)} (h : sSupIndep 𝒩) (htop : sSup 𝒩 = ⊤) :
    DirectSum.IsInternal (fun N : ↥𝒩 ↦ (N : Submodule R M)) :=
  (DirectSum.isInternal_submodule_iff_iSupIndep_and_iSup_eq_top _).mpr
    ⟨(sSupIndep_iff _).mp h, by rwa [← sSup_eq_iSup']⟩

end Transport

section Split

variable {R : Type*} [Ring R] {M : Type*} [AddCommGroup M] [Module R M]

/-- The image in `M` of `p.submoduleOf q` is `p` again, when `p ≤ q`. -/
private lemma map_subtype_submoduleOf {p q : Submodule R M} (hpq : p ≤ q) :
    Submodule.map q.subtype (p.submoduleOf q) = p := by
  rw [Submodule.submoduleOf, Submodule.map_comap_subtype, inf_eq_right.mpr hpq]

/-- If `C` is a complement of `p` inside `q`, the quotient `↥q ⧸ p.submoduleOf q` is linearly
isomorphic to `C`. -/
private lemma nonempty_quotient_submoduleOf_equiv {p q C : Submodule R M}
    (hd : Disjoint p C) (hsup : p ⊔ C = q) :
    Nonempty ((↥q ⧸ p.submoduleOf q) ≃ₗ[R] ↥C) := by
  have hpq : p ≤ q := hsup ▸ le_sup_left
  have hCq : C ≤ q := hsup ▸ le_sup_right
  have hcompl : IsCompl (p.submoduleOf q) (C.submoduleOf q) := by
    constructor
    · rw [Submodule.disjoint_def]
      exact fun z hz1 hz2 ↦ Subtype.ext (by simpa using Submodule.disjoint_def.mp hd _ hz1 hz2)
    · rw [codisjoint_iff, eq_top_iff]
      intro z _
      have hz : (z : M) ∈ p ⊔ C := hsup ▸ z.2
      obtain ⟨a, ha, c, hc, hac⟩ := Submodule.mem_sup.mp hz
      exact Submodule.mem_sup.mpr
        ⟨⟨a, hpq ha⟩, ha, ⟨c, hCq hc⟩, hc, Subtype.ext (by simpa using hac)⟩
  exact ⟨(Submodule.quotientEquivOfIsCompl _ _ hcompl).trans
    (Submodule.comapSubtypeEquivOfLe hCq)⟩

/-- A submodule with projective quotient splits off: if `p ≤ q` are submodules of `M` and the
quotient `↥q ⧸ p.submoduleOf q` is projective, then `p` has a complement inside `q`, namely a
submodule `C ≤ q` with `Disjoint p C` and `p ⊔ C = q`, linearly isomorphic to that quotient. -/
private lemma exists_isCompl_of_projective_quotient {p q : Submodule R M} (hpq : p ≤ q)
    [Module.Projective R (↥q ⧸ p.submoduleOf q)] :
    ∃ C : Submodule R M, Disjoint p C ∧ p ⊔ C = q ∧
      Nonempty (↥C ≃ₗ[R] (↥q ⧸ p.submoduleOf q)) := by
  obtain ⟨s, hs⟩ := Module.projective_lifting_property (p.submoduleOf q).mkQ
    LinearMap.id (p.submoduleOf q).mkQ_surjective
  have hs' : ∀ y, (p.submoduleOf q).mkQ (s y) = y := fun y ↦ by
    simpa using LinearMap.congr_fun hs y
  have hD : IsCompl (p.submoduleOf q) (LinearMap.range s) := by
    constructor
    · rw [Submodule.disjoint_def]
      rintro z hz ⟨y, rfl⟩
      have hy : y = 0 := (hs' y).symm.trans ((Submodule.Quotient.mk_eq_zero _).mpr hz)
      simp [hy]
    · rw [codisjoint_iff, eq_top_iff]
      intro z _
      rw [show z = z - s ((p.submoduleOf q).mkQ z) + s ((p.submoduleOf q).mkQ z) by abel]
      refine Submodule.add_mem_sup ((Submodule.Quotient.mk_eq_zero _).mp ?_) ⟨_, rfl⟩
      rw [← Submodule.mkQ_apply, map_sub, hs', sub_self]
  refine ⟨(LinearMap.range s).map q.subtype, ?_, ?_, ⟨?_⟩⟩
  · rw [Submodule.disjoint_def]
    rintro z hzp ⟨w, hw, rfl⟩
    simp [Submodule.disjoint_def.mp hD.1 w hzp hw]
  · have hsup : Submodule.map q.subtype (p.submoduleOf q ⊔ LinearMap.range s) = q := by
      rw [hD.sup_eq_top, Submodule.map_top, Submodule.range_subtype]
    rwa [Submodule.map_sup, map_subtype_submoduleOf hpq] at hsup
  · exact (Submodule.equivMapOfInjective q.subtype (Submodule.injective_subtype q)
      (LinearMap.range s)).symm.trans (Submodule.quotientEquivOfIsCompl _ _ hD).symm

/-- Adding a submodule disjoint from the sum of an independent family keeps it independent. -/
private lemma sSupIndep_insert_of_disjoint {𝒩 : Set (Submodule R M)} (h : sSupIndep 𝒩)
    {C : Submodule R M} (hC : Disjoint C (sSup 𝒩)) : sSupIndep (insert C 𝒩) := by
  intro a ha
  rcases eq_or_ne a C with rfl | hne
  · refine hC.mono_right (sSup_le fun N hN ↦ le_sSup ?_)
    exact (Set.mem_insert_iff.mp hN.1).resolve_left fun hNa ↦ hN.2 (by simp [hNa])
  · have haN : a ∈ 𝒩 := (Set.mem_insert_iff.mp ha).resolve_left hne
    rw [Set.insert_sdiff_of_notMem _ (by simpa using hne.symm), sSup_insert,
      Submodule.disjoint_def]
    intro z hza hzsup
    obtain ⟨c, hc, n, hn, rfl⟩ := Submodule.mem_sup.mp hzsup
    have hn' : n ∈ sSup 𝒩 :=
      (sSup_le_sSup Set.sdiff_subset : sSup (𝒩 \ {a}) ≤ sSup 𝒩) hn
    have hc0 : c = 0 :=
      Submodule.disjoint_def.mp hC c hc (by simpa using sub_mem (le_sSup haN hza) hn')
    simpa [hc0] using Submodule.disjoint_def.mp (h haN) (c + n) hza (by simpa [hc0] using hn)

/-- The union of a chain of independent sets of submodules is independent. -/
private lemma sSupIndep_sUnion_of_isChain {c : Set (Set (Submodule R M))}
    (hc : IsChain (· ⊆ ·) c) (h : ∀ s ∈ c, sSupIndep s) : sSupIndep (⋃₀ c) :=
  iSupIndep_sUnion_of_directed hc.directedOn h

end Split

section Good

variable {R : Type u} [CommRing R] {M : Type v} [AddCommGroup M] [Module R M]
  {S : Type w} [CommRing S] [Algebra R S]

/-- Base change of submodules commutes with arbitrary sums. -/
private lemma baseChange_sSup (𝒩 : Set (Submodule R M)) :
    (sSup 𝒩).baseChange S = ⨆ N ∈ 𝒩, N.baseChange S := by
  refine le_antisymm ?_ (iSup₂_le fun N hN ↦ Submodule.baseChange_mono _ (le_sSup hN))
  rw [Submodule.baseChange_eq_span, Submodule.span_le]
  rintro y ⟨m, hm, rfl⟩
  have key : sSup 𝒩 ≤ Submodule.comap (TensorProduct.mk R S M 1)
      ((⨆ N ∈ 𝒩, N.baseChange S).restrictScalars R) := by
    refine sSup_le fun N hN z hz ↦ ?_
    simp only [Submodule.mem_comap, Submodule.restrictScalars_mem, TensorProduct.mk_apply]
    exact le_iSup₂ (f := fun (N : Submodule R M) (_ : N ∈ 𝒩) ↦ N.baseChange S) N hN
      (Submodule.tmul_mem_baseChange_of_mem 1 hz)
  exact key hm

/-- Base change of submodules commutes with binary sums. -/
private lemma baseChange_sup (p q : Submodule R M) :
    (p ⊔ q).baseChange S = p.baseChange S ⊔ q.baseChange S := by
  simpa only [sSup_pair, iSup_pair] using baseChange_sSup (S := S) {p, q}

/-- If an `R`-module is countably generated then so is its base change, over the new ring. -/
private lemma countablyGenerated_baseChange (h : Module.CountablyGenerated R M) :
    Module.CountablyGenerated S (S ⊗[R] M) := by
  obtain ⟨t, ht, hspan⟩ := h
  refine ⟨TensorProduct.mk R S M 1 '' t, ht.image _, ?_⟩
  rw [← Submodule.baseChange_span, hspan, Submodule.baseChange_top]

/-- The countably generated quotient produced at a capture step: if `q = p ⊔ span R t` with
`p ≤ q` and `t` a countable subset of `q`, the quotient `↥q ⧸ p.submoduleOf q` is countably
generated. -/
private lemma countablyGenerated_quotient_of_sup_span {p q : Submodule R M} (hpq : p ≤ q)
    {t : Set M} (ht : t.Countable) (htq : t ⊆ q) (hq : q = p ⊔ Submodule.span R t) :
    Module.CountablyGenerated R (↥q ⧸ p.submoduleOf q) := by
  classical
  have himg : q.subtype '' (q.subtype ⁻¹' t) = t :=
    Set.image_preimage_eq_of_subset fun y hy ↦ ⟨⟨y, htq hy⟩, rfl⟩
  refine ⟨(p.submoduleOf q).mkQ '' (q.subtype ⁻¹' t),
    (ht.preimage (Submodule.injective_subtype q)).image _, ?_⟩
  rw [← Submodule.map_span, Submodule.map_mkQ_eq_top]
  apply Submodule.map_injective_of_injective (Submodule.injective_subtype q)
  rw [Submodule.map_sup, Submodule.map_span, himg, Submodule.map_top, Submodule.range_subtype,
    map_subtype_submoduleOf hpq, ← hq]

/-- The range of a base change is the base change of the range.  This needs no flatness
hypothesis: base change is right exact. -/
private lemma range_baseChange {N : Type*} [AddCommGroup N] [Module R N] (f : N →ₗ[R] M) :
    LinearMap.range (LinearMap.baseChange S f) = (LinearMap.range f).baseChange S := by
  refine le_antisymm ?_ ?_
  · rintro _ ⟨y, rfl⟩
    induction y with
    | tmul a x =>
        rw [LinearMap.baseChange_tmul]
        exact Submodule.tmul_mem_baseChange_of_mem a ⟨x, rfl⟩
    | add x y hx hy => simpa only [map_add] using add_mem hx hy
  · rw [Submodule.baseChange_eq_span, Submodule.span_le]
    rintro _ ⟨m, ⟨x, rfl⟩, rfl⟩
    exact ⟨1 ⊗ₜ[R] x, by simp⟩

/-- Flat base change of a quotient of nested submodules: for `p ≤ q` submodules of `M` and `S`
flat over `R`, base change carries `↥q ⧸ p.submoduleOf q` to
`↥(q.baseChange S) ⧸ (p.baseChange S).submoduleOf (q.baseChange S)`. -/
private lemma nonempty_baseChange_quotient_equiv [Module.Flat R S] {p q : Submodule R M}
    (hpq : p ≤ q) :
    Nonempty (S ⊗[R] (↥q ⧸ p.submoduleOf q) ≃ₗ[S]
      (↥(q.baseChange S) ⧸ (p.baseChange S).submoduleOf (q.baseChange S))) := by
  have hcomp : (q.baseChange S).subtype ∘ₗ
        (((Submodule.toBaseChange.toLinearEquiv S q :
              S ⊗[R] ↥q ≃ₗ[S] ↥(q.baseChange S)) : S ⊗[R] ↥q →ₗ[S] ↥(q.baseChange S)) ∘ₗ
          TensorProduct.AlgebraTensorModule.lTensor S S
            ((p.submoduleOf q).subtype.restrictScalars R))
      = LinearMap.baseChange S (q.subtype ∘ₗ (p.submoduleOf q).subtype) := by
    refine LinearMap.ext fun y ↦ ?_
    induction y with
    | tmul a z => simp
    | add x y hx hy => simp only [map_add, hx, hy]
  have hmap : Submodule.map
      ((Submodule.toBaseChange.toLinearEquiv S q :
          S ⊗[R] ↥q ≃ₗ[S] ↥(q.baseChange S)) : S ⊗[R] ↥q →ₗ[S] ↥(q.baseChange S))
      (LinearMap.range (TensorProduct.AlgebraTensorModule.lTensor S S
        ((p.submoduleOf q).subtype.restrictScalars R)))
      = (p.baseChange S).submoduleOf (q.baseChange S) := by
    apply Submodule.map_injective_of_injective
      (Submodule.injective_subtype (q.baseChange S))
    rw [map_subtype_submoduleOf (Submodule.baseChange_mono _ hpq), ← LinearMap.range_comp,
      ← LinearMap.range_comp, hcomp, range_baseChange, LinearMap.range_comp,
      Submodule.range_subtype, map_subtype_submoduleOf hpq]
  exact ⟨(TensorProduct.AlgebraTensorModule.tensorQuotientEquiv S R S (p.submoduleOf q)).trans
    (Submodule.Quotient.equiv _ _ (Submodule.toBaseChange.toLinearEquiv S q) hmap)⟩

end Good

section Family

variable {R : Type u} [CommRing R] {M : Type v} [AddCommGroup M] [Module R M]
  {S : Type w} [CommRing S] [Algebra R S] {I : Type*} [DecidableEq I]
  {Q : I → Submodule S (S ⊗[R] M)}

/-- In an internal direct sum, membership in the subsum indexed by `J` forces the support of the
decomposition to lie in `J`. This is the converse of `mem_iSup_of_support_subset`. -/
private lemma support_subset_of_mem_iSup [DirectSum.Decomposition Q] (J : Set I)
    {x : S ⊗[R] M} (hx : x ∈ ⨆ i ∈ J, Q i) :
    ∀ i, (DirectSum.decompose Q x) i ≠ 0 → i ∈ J := by
  classical
  intro i hi
  by_contra hiJ
  refine hi ?_
  have hle : (⨆ j ∈ J, Q j) ≤ LinearMap.ker
      ((DirectSum.component S I (fun j ↦ ↥(Q j)) i).comp
        (DirectSum.decomposeLinearEquiv Q).toLinearMap) := by
    refine iSup₂_le fun j hj y hy ↦ ?_
    simp only [LinearMap.mem_ker, LinearMap.coe_comp, Function.comp_apply, LinearEquiv.coe_coe,
      DirectSum.decomposeLinearEquiv_apply, ← DirectSum.apply_eq_component]
    rw [DirectSum.decompose_coe Q (⟨y, hy⟩ : ↥(Q j))]
    exact DirectSum.of_eq_of_ne j i _ fun h ↦ hiJ (h ▸ hj)
  simpa only [LinearMap.mem_ker, LinearMap.coe_comp, Function.comp_apply, LinearEquiv.coe_coe,
    DirectSum.decomposeLinearEquiv_apply, ← DirectSum.apply_eq_component] using hle hx

/-- Subsums of an internal direct sum indexed by disjoint sets of indices are disjoint. -/
private lemma disjoint_iSup_of_disjoint [DirectSum.Decomposition Q] {X Y : Set I}
    (h : Disjoint X Y) : Disjoint (⨆ i ∈ X, Q i) (⨆ i ∈ Y, Q i) := by
  classical
  rw [Submodule.disjoint_def]
  intro z hzX hzY
  have hz0 : ∀ i, (DirectSum.decompose Q z) i = 0 := fun i ↦ by
    by_contra hne
    exact Set.disjoint_left.mp h (support_subset_of_mem_iSup X hzX i hne)
      (support_subset_of_mem_iSup Y hzY i hne)
  have hzz : (DirectSum.decomposeLinearEquiv Q) z = 0 := by
    refine DFinsupp.ext fun i ↦ ?_
    simpa [DirectSum.decomposeLinearEquiv_apply] using hz0 i
  simpa using (DirectSum.decomposeLinearEquiv Q).map_eq_zero_iff.mp hzz

/-- Every subsum of an internal direct sum decomposition of a projective module is projective,
being a direct summand: its complement is the subsum over the complementary set of indices. -/
private lemma projective_iSup [DirectSum.Decomposition Q] [Module.Projective S (S ⊗[R] M)]
    (hQtop : (⨆ i, Q i) = ⊤) (K : Set I) : Module.Projective S ↥(⨆ i ∈ K, Q i) := by
  have hcompl : IsCompl (⨆ i ∈ K, Q i) (⨆ i ∈ Kᶜ, Q i) := by
    refine ⟨disjoint_iSup_of_disjoint disjoint_compl_right, ?_⟩
    rw [codisjoint_iff, eq_top_iff, ← hQtop]
    refine iSup_le fun i ↦ ?_
    by_cases hi : i ∈ K
    · exact le_sup_of_le_left (le_iSup₂_of_le i hi le_rfl)
    · exact le_sup_of_le_right (le_iSup₂_of_le i hi le_rfl)
  exact Module.Projective.of_split (R := S) (P := ↥(⨆ i ∈ K, Q i)) (M := S ⊗[R] M)
    (⨆ i ∈ K, Q i).subtype (Submodule.projectionOnto _ _ hcompl)
    (by ext x; exact congrArg Subtype.val (Submodule.projectionOnto_apply_left hcompl x))

/-- The base change of the quotient of two good submodules is projective: over `S` it is the
subsum of the decomposition over the indices in the larger index set and not the smaller, which
is a direct summand of `S ⊗[R] M`. -/
private lemma projective_baseChange_quotient [DirectSum.Decomposition Q]
    [Module.Projective S (S ⊗[R] M)] [Module.Flat R S] (hQtop : (⨆ i, Q i) = ⊤)
    {p q : Submodule R M} (hpq : p ≤ q) {X Y : Set I}
    (hp : p.baseChange S = ⨆ i ∈ X, Q i) (hq : q.baseChange S = ⨆ i ∈ Y, Q i) :
    Module.Projective S (S ⊗[R] (↥q ⧸ p.submoduleOf q)) := by
  classical
  have hdisj : Disjoint (p.baseChange S) (⨆ i ∈ Y \ X, Q i) := by
    rw [hp]
    exact disjoint_iSup_of_disjoint disjoint_sdiff_self_right
  have hsupW : p.baseChange S ⊔ (⨆ i ∈ Y \ X, Q i) = q.baseChange S := by
    rw [hp, ← iSup_union, Set.union_sdiff_self, iSup_union, ← hp, ← hq]
    exact sup_eq_right.mpr (Submodule.baseChange_mono _ hpq)
  have hWproj : Module.Projective S ↥(⨆ i ∈ Y \ X, Q i) := projective_iSup hQtop (Y \ X)
  obtain ⟨e⟩ := nonempty_quotient_submoduleOf_equiv (R := S) (M := S ⊗[R] M) hdisj hsupW
  have hquot : Module.Projective S
      (↥(q.baseChange S) ⧸ (p.baseChange S).submoduleOf (q.baseChange S)) :=
    projective_of_linearEquiv e.symm
  obtain ⟨f⟩ := nonempty_baseChange_quotient_equiv (S := S) hpq
  exact projective_of_linearEquiv f.symm

omit [DecidableEq I] in
/-- If the base change of every member of `𝒩` is a subsum of `Q`, so is that of `sSup 𝒩`. -/
private lemma exists_baseChange_sSup_eq_iSup {𝒩 : Set (Submodule R M)}
    (h : ∀ N ∈ 𝒩, ∃ J : Set I, N.baseChange S = ⨆ i ∈ J, Q i) :
    ∃ J : Set I, (sSup 𝒩).baseChange S = ⨆ i ∈ J, Q i := by
  choose J hJ using h
  refine ⟨⋃ N : ↥𝒩, J N N.2, ?_⟩
  rw [baseChange_sSup, iSup_iUnion]
  refine le_antisymm (iSup₂_le fun N hN ↦ ?_) (iSup_le fun N ↦ ?_)
  · exact le_iSup_of_le ⟨N, hN⟩ (hJ N hN).le
  · exact le_iSup₂_of_le (N : Submodule R M) N.2 (hJ N N.2).ge

end Family

section Main

variable {R : Type u} [CommRing R] {M : Type v} [AddCommGroup M] [Module R M]
  {S : Type w} [CommRing S] [Algebra R S]

/-- The capture step: given a submodule `p` whose base change is a subsum of the decomposition
`Q` of `S ⊗[R] M` into countably generated summands, and an element `x`, there is a countably
generated projective submodule `C` disjoint from `p` with `x ∈ p ⊔ C`, and the base change of
`p ⊔ C` is again a subsum of `Q`. -/
private lemma exists_projective_disjoint_of_baseChange_eq_iSup [Module.FaithfullyFlat R S]
    [Module.Projective S (S ⊗[R] M)] {I : Type*} [DecidableEq I]
    {Q : I → Submodule S (S ⊗[R] M)} [DirectSum.Decomposition Q] (hQ : DirectSum.IsInternal Q)
    (hQtop : (⨆ i, Q i) = ⊤) (hQcg : ∀ i, (Q i).IsCountablyGenerated) {p : Submodule R M}
    {J : Set I} (hJ : p.baseChange S = ⨆ i ∈ J, Q i) (x : M) :
    ∃ C : Submodule R M, Disjoint p C ∧ x ∈ p ⊔ C ∧
      (Module.CountablyGenerated R ↥C ∧ Module.Projective R ↥C) ∧
      ∃ K : Set I, (p ⊔ C).baseChange S = ⨆ i ∈ K, Q i := by
  -- Capture `x` in a good countably generated submodule (Lemma 05A8).
  obtain ⟨N', I', ⟨t, ht, htspan⟩, hxN', hN'⟩ :=
    exists_countablyGenerated_baseChange_eq_iSup_of_internal_directSum
      (R := R) (S := S) (M := M) Q hQ hQcg (Submodule.span R {x})
      ⟨{x}, Set.countable_singleton x, rfl⟩
  set q := p ⊔ N' with hq
  have hpq : p ≤ q := le_sup_left
  have hqgood : q.baseChange S = ⨆ i ∈ (J ∪ I'), Q i := by
    rw [hq, baseChange_sup, hJ, hN', iSup_coe_set, iSup_union]
  have htq : t ⊆ q := by
    rw [hq]
    exact fun y hy ↦ Submodule.mem_sup_right (htspan ▸ Submodule.subset_span hy)
  have hcgq : Module.CountablyGenerated R (↥q ⧸ p.submoduleOf q) :=
    countablyGenerated_quotient_of_sup_span hpq ht htq (by rw [hq, htspan])
  have : Module.Projective S (S ⊗[R] (↥q ⧸ p.submoduleOf q)) :=
    projective_baseChange_quotient hQtop hpq hJ hqgood
  have : Module.Projective R (↥q ⧸ p.submoduleOf q) :=
    (Module.countablyGenerated_and_projective_of_faithfullyFlat_baseChange (S := S)
      (countablyGenerated_baseChange hcgq)).2
  obtain ⟨C, hCdisj, hCsup, ⟨eC⟩⟩ := exists_isCompl_of_projective_quotient hpq
  refine ⟨C, hCdisj, ?_,
    ⟨countablyGenerated_of_linearEquiv eC.symm hcgq, projective_of_linearEquiv eC.symm⟩,
    J ∪ I', by rwa [hCsup]⟩
  rw [hCsup, hq]
  exact Submodule.mem_sup_right (hxN' (Submodule.mem_span_singleton_self x))

/-- The heart of the descent: if `S ⊗[R] M` is projective over `S` for a faithfully flat
`R`-algebra `S`, then `M` is the internal direct sum of projective `R`-submodules. -/
private theorem exists_sSupIndep_projective_of_projective_baseChange
    [Module.FaithfullyFlat R S] [Module.Projective S (S ⊗[R] M)] :
    ∃ 𝒩 : Set (Submodule R M), sSupIndep 𝒩 ∧ sSup 𝒩 = ⊤ ∧
      ∀ N ∈ 𝒩, Module.Projective R ↥N := by
  classical
  -- Kaplansky's theorem (058Y) for the projective `S`-module `S ⊗[R] M`.
  obtain ⟨𝒬, h𝒬ind, h𝒬top, h𝒬⟩ :=
    Submodule.exists_sSupIndep_countable_span_projective_of_projective
      (R := S) (M := S ⊗[R] M)
  set Q : ↥𝒬 → Submodule S (S ⊗[R] M) := fun i ↦ (i : Submodule S (S ⊗[R] M)) with hQdef
  have hQtop : (⨆ i, Q i) = ⊤ := by rwa [hQdef, ← sSup_eq_iSup']
  have hQinternal : DirectSum.IsInternal Q := isInternal_of_sSupIndep h𝒬ind h𝒬top
  let _ : DirectSum.Decomposition Q := hQinternal.chooseDecomposition Q
  set 𝒞 : Set (Set (Submodule R M)) :=
    {𝒩 | sSupIndep 𝒩 ∧ (∀ N ∈ 𝒩, Module.CountablyGenerated R ↥N ∧ Module.Projective R ↥N) ∧
      ∃ J : Set ↥𝒬, (sSup 𝒩).baseChange S = ⨆ i ∈ J, Q i} with h𝒞def
  obtain ⟨𝒩, h𝒩max⟩ : ∃ 𝒩, Maximal (· ∈ 𝒞) 𝒩 := by
    refine zorn_subset 𝒞 fun c hc hchain ↦ ⟨⋃₀ c, ⟨?_, ?_, ?_⟩,
      fun s hs ↦ Set.subset_sUnion_of_mem hs⟩
    · exact sSupIndep_sUnion_of_isChain hchain fun s hs ↦ (hc hs).1
    · exact fun N ⟨s, hs, hN⟩ ↦ (hc hs).2.1 N hN
    · rw [sSup_sUnion, ← sSup_image]
      exact exists_baseChange_sSup_eq_iSup (Set.forall_mem_image.2 fun s hs ↦ (hc hs).2.2)
  obtain ⟨⟨hind, hmem, J, hJ⟩, hmax⟩ := h𝒩max
  refine ⟨𝒩, hind, ?_, fun N hN ↦ (hmem N hN).2⟩
  by_contra hne
  obtain ⟨x, -, hx⟩ := SetLike.exists_of_lt (lt_top_iff_ne_top.mpr hne)
  obtain ⟨C, hCdisj, hxC, hC, hgood⟩ := exists_projective_disjoint_of_baseChange_eq_iSup
    hQinternal hQtop (fun i ↦ (h𝒬 _ i.2).1) hJ x
  have hCnotmem : C ∉ 𝒩 := fun hC ↦ hx (sup_le le_rfl (le_sSup hC) hxC)
  have hins : insert C 𝒩 ∈ 𝒞 := by
    refine ⟨sSupIndep_insert_of_disjoint hind hCdisj.symm, ?_, ?_⟩
    · rintro N (rfl | hN)
      exacts [hC, hmem N hN]
    · rwa [sSup_insert, sup_comm]
  exact hCnotmem (hmax hins (Set.subset_insert _ _) (Set.mem_insert _ _))

end Main

open scoped TensorProduct in
/-- **Stacks 05A9.** Let `R → S` be a faithfully flat ring map and let `M` be an `R`-module. If
the `S`-module `S ⊗_R M` is projective, then `M` is projective. -/
@[sga "3 VIB 11.8.1" "(ii)", sga "3 VIB 11.8.2", sga "3 VIB 11.9.1" "(iii) ⇒ (ii), final step"]
public theorem solution {R : Type*} [CommRing R] {M : Type*}
    [AddCommGroup M] [Module R M] (S : Type*) [CommRing S] [Algebra R S]
    [Module.FaithfullyFlat R S] [Module.Projective S (S ⊗[R] M)] :
    Module.Projective R M := by
  classical
  obtain ⟨𝒩, hind, htop, hproj⟩ :=
    exists_sSupIndep_projective_of_projective_baseChange (R := R) (M := M) (S := S)
  have hinternal := isInternal_of_sSupIndep hind htop
  have : ∀ N : ↥𝒩, Module.Projective R ↥(N : Submodule R M) := fun N ↦ hproj N N.2
  exact projective_of_linearEquiv (LinearEquiv.ofBijective (DirectSum.coeLinearMap _) hinternal)

end Stacks.Projective.FaithfullyFlatDescent

/-!
# Faithfully flat descent of projectivity

Let `R → S` be a faithfully flat ring map and let `M` be an `R`-module. If the base change of `M`
to `S` is a projective `S`-module, then `M` is a projective `R`-module. No finiteness of any kind
is assumed of `M`.

The source's argument builds a Kaplansky dévissage of `M`, that is, a well-ordered increasing
filtration `(Mα)` of `M` by submodules, continuous at limits, with every quotient
`M(α+1)/Mα` countably generated and projective; such a filtration exhibits `M` as the direct sum
of its successive quotients (Lemma 058V), so `M` is projective as a direct sum of projectives.
The filtration is produced by transfinite recursion on a well-ordering of `M`: writing
`M ⊗_R S = ⨁_{i ∈ I} Q_i` as a direct sum of countably generated `S`-modules (Theorem 058Y, the
Kaplansky theorem for projective modules), one arranges that each `Mα ⊗_R S` is the sum of a
subfamily of the `Q_i`. At a successor step the smallest `x ∉ Mα` is captured by a countably
generated `R`-submodule `P ⊆ M/Mα` whose base change is again a subsum of the `Q_i` (Lemma 05A8);
that base change is a direct summand of the projective module `M ⊗_R S`, hence projective, so `P`
is projective by the countably generated case of the present theorem (Lemma 05A6), and
`M(α+1)` is taken to be the preimage of `P`. Flatness of `S` over `R` is what makes
`(M/Mα) ⊗_R S = (M ⊗_R S)/(Mα ⊗_R S)` and `P ⊗_R S` its image in `M ⊗_R S`; faithful flatness
enters only through Lemma 05A6.

## Main results

* `Module.Projective.of_projective_tensorProduct`: projectivity descends along a faithfully flat
  ring map.

## Implementation notes

The statement is the exact analogue for projectivity of Mathlib's
`Module.Flat.of_flat_tensorProduct`, descent of flatness along a faithfully flat ring map, and is
spelled the same way: the base change is the concrete tensor product `S ⊗[R] M` with its canonical
`S`-action on the left factor, the ring map is `[Algebra R S]`, faithful flatness of it is
`[Module.FaithfullyFlat R S]` (equivalently `(algebraMap R S).FaithfullyFlat`, by
`RingHom.faithfullyFlat_algebraMap_iff`), and `S` is an explicit argument, since it does not occur
in the conclusion and so cannot be inferred at a call site. Following that lemma and the
neighbouring statements of Tags 0GVD, 05A3, 05A5 and 05A6, the base change factor is written on the left where
the source writes `M ⊗_R S`; the two are identified by `TensorProduct.comm`, and only the
left-hand order carries an `S`-module structure in Mathlib.

This theorem strictly contains the two cases of it already in this repository, and supersedes
neither name: `Module.Projective.of_finite_of_isBaseChange_of_faithfullyFlat` (Proposition 058S)
adds `Module.Finite S N`, and Lemma 05A6's
`Module.countablyGenerated_and_projective_of_faithfullyFlat_baseChange` adds
`Module.CountablyGenerated S (S ⊗[R] M)` and concludes countable generation of `M` too. Both
are stated where the source states them, in earlier sections, and both are inputs to the proof
of this one rather than corollaries of it, so the unqualified name is this theorem's; the
qualified names record the hypotheses their own arguments need. Descent of projectivity in this
unrestricted form is what the descent of formal smoothness, Lemma 06CM, requires of `Ω[S⁄R]`,
which is not of finite presentation in general.

Faithfulness. (1) No hypothesis is dead: `Module.Projective S (S ⊗[R] M)` is the sole input to the
conclusion, and faithful flatness cannot be weakened to flatness, since for `R = ℤ`, `S = ℚ` and
`M = ℚ` the map `ℤ → ℚ` is flat and not faithfully flat, `ℚ ⊗_ℤ ℚ ≅ ℚ` is free of rank one over
`ℚ` hence projective, and `ℚ` is not a projective `ℤ`-module. (2) The scalars and the base are the
source's: the hypothesis is projectivity of `S ⊗[R] M` over `S`, not over `R`, the conclusion is
projectivity of `M` over `R`, and the tensor product is over `R` along `algebraMap R S`. (3) Where
the source names a canonical map the statement pins it: the `S`-action on `S ⊗[R] M` is the
canonical one on the left factor, not an unspecified module structure making `S ⊗[R] M` a base
change of `M`, and faithful flatness is that of `S` as an `R`-module along `algebraMap R S`.
(4) The instantiation is not degenerate, and not covered by either special case above: with
`R = ZMod 6`, `S = R × R` — free of rank two, hence faithfully flat — `I` an uncountable index set
and `M = ⨁_{i ∈ I} (3)` the direct sum of copies of the ideal `(3) ⊆ ZMod 6`, the base change
`S ⊗[R] M` is projective over `S`, while `M` is neither finite nor countably generated nor free,
`(3)` being a direct summand of `ZMod 6` with two elements. Nor is the hypothesis automatic: for
`R = ℤ`, `S = ℤ × ℤ` faithfully flat and `M = ℚ`, the module `S ⊗_ℤ ℚ ≅ ℚ × ℚ` is not projective
over `ℤ × ℤ`, matching the failure of the conclusion.

